As part of a side discussion with Rast, I created an Excel spreadsheet
that duplicates the nethack code for spell success (and failure) rates.
I am looking for some testers / reviewers to ensure the calculations
are correct. Please download
  http://homepage.mac.com/mhjohnson/nh-spells.xls
for a copy of the spreadsheet. To explain what is there / how to use
it:

The "code" worksheet has a copy of the nethack code from spells.c

The StatRole worksheet has a copy of the factors used in the
calculations (by role), the names of the spell skill levels, and a list
of the spells, the spell family, and level of spell.

The Equiv worksheet does the calculations based on three selectable
items plus some values on the left side to indicate the armor being
used. The selectable items are:
 - the Role (e.g., Valkyrie or Wizard)
 - the Spell (e.g., Force Bolt)
 - the spell skill level (e.g., Unskilled)
Note - there are no checks that the skill level is not exceeded for the
family of the spell selected.

Rows 17 through 23 refer to the type of armor used (suit, cloak,
footwear, gloves, helmet, shield, and shirt). The values that are used
in column B for those rows are:
 blank - no armor (or armor that doesn't affect spell rates)
 R - robe (cloak only - improves spell rates)
 M - metallic armor (all but cloak & shirt)
 S - small shield (shield only)
 any other letter for shield will indicate a shield that is heavier
than a small shield

In doing tests with the worksheet, I noticed that the spell rates
improve on higher level spell to the same cap as lower level spells as
XL and spell skill increase. The cap on success rates for spells is
dependent on role, the Int (or Wiz) stat, the armor being worn, and in
some cases the type of spell (e.g., "healing" or "special"). A few
roles (e.g., B or V) appear to require a robe to get to 0% failure for
even level 1 spells.

I am also interested in identifying the ranges of improvement /
reduction of spell success rates. For example, a robe appears to
improve success rates between 40% and 60%.

Again, let me know if this has any problems or if it is helpful to you.

